From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:59:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Also keep media elements alive while they load or
 seek

Upstream #48979 keeps a media element alive through its owner document
while it plays or is eligible for autoplay, and #49367 lets it go after
it has ended. An element that delays the load event or seeks is still
collected at the next garbage collection once script drops it, which
stops it and lets the load event fire early, and the keep-alive is only
updated at a few points of the media element's algorithms.

Like Gecko, and Blink apart from autoplay, also keep a media element
alive while it delays the load event and, with a resource and no error,
while it seeks. Don't keep an element without a resource alive, and
keep one that is eligible for autoplay only until it has enough data:
then it either plays or never will. Update the keep-alive whenever the
network state, paused, seeking, the can autoplay flag, the error, the
ready state or the delaying-the-load-event flag changes.

Fork note: meant for upstream; found with the QuickJS backend, which
frees such elements as soon as script drops them, but SpiderMonkey loses
them too once a GC runs. Until the 2026-10-29 sync this patch had its
own keep-alive set; it now extends upstream's. Besides the upstream
change, this drops the QuickJS conditions in the expectations of
autoplay-disabled-by-permissions-policy; patch 49 dropped the others.
Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: Adds a WPT test that unreferenced media elements keep playing
across a garbage collection.

Validation: Release builds of upstream f040186a7ee with SpiderMonkey, with and without the series, `./mach test-wpt --processes 4` of html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, the-video-element, the-audio-element, infrastructure/assumptions/allowed-to-play.html, webaudio/the-audio-api/the-mediaelementaudiosourcenode-interface and 2 _mozilla media tests: the new test passes 7 of 7 (harness timeout without), autoplay-disabled-by-permissions-policy and played-loop as in the updated expectations (autoplay-disabled timed out intermittently before, upstream issues #46012 and #46804); the remaining differences are track timeouts and track-cues-sorted-before-dispatch failures that both builds show intermittently, and one seek-to-max-value failure in 7 runs with the series (`currentTime` 0 right after setting it), not reproduced in 6 reruns, apart from load-removes-queued-error-event, which now hangs whenever GStreamer 1.24.2's playbin3 stalls on a `<video>` with an audio-only MP3: 4 of 6 runs on this host without the series too; the stalled element delays the load event, and before, only a garbage collection that freed it let the load event fire (upstream's intermittent tracker has 9 unexpected results for that test in total). The three tests that fail on QuickJS CI pass with forced GCs every 20 ms too. With logging of the keep-alive updates, every element in direct checks stopped being kept alive once it had been loaded and never played, played to the end, paused, replayed after the end without a seekable range, or played backwards to the start. An independent agent review found no blockers; its two should-fix findings for this patch (ended but unpaused elements were kept alive; changes of the error went through no hook) are fixed. QuickJS release build of this stack: the new tests and the media tests that had QuickJS expectations (autoplay.html, allowed-to-play.html, resource-selection-candidate-moved.html and the three autoplay permissions policy tests) give SpiderMonkey's results in 2 of 2 runs; without the series they time out as the dropped expectations said. 2026-10-29 sync, reworked onto upstream's keep-alive: release builds of this sync's stack and of plain upstream ed6a9248aae, `./mach test-wpt --release` of the media-elements, the-video-element and the-audio-element directories with fetch, xhr, eventsource, cors, subresource-integrity, resource-timing, canvas image drawing and _mozilla/memory (2,247 tests), then 3 reruns on both of every test whose results differed: the same results on both except for intermittent ones, gc-unreferenced-media-element and upstream's playing_media_no_gc pass; a playing element's back/forward cache restore fails on both (upstream #48979 pauses it at unload). DAYU200 (OpenHarmony 7.0), headless release builds with SpiderMonkey and with QuickJS, `./mach test-wpt --ohos --processes 1 --timeout-multiplier 2` of the media series' tests, loading-the-media-resource, error-codes and playing-the-media-resource (75 tests): all as expected but the two autoplay permissions policy tests that pass where a timeout is expected, on both engines; no crash.
